    "category": "Endocrine & Hormones",
    "description": "Androstenedione is one of the primary androgens produced in the ovaries of women, and, to a lesser degree, in the adrenal glands of both men and women. It is converted to estrone by adipose tissue and the liver. Estrone is a form of estrogen that is of relatively low potency, compared with estradiol. In premenopausal women, estrone levels are relatively small in comparison to estradiol levels. However, in children and postmenopausal women, estrone is a major estrogen source. If, for some reason, androstenedione production is increased, a child may experience premature sexual development. In postmenopausal woman, increased androstenedione production may result in bleeding, endometriosis, ovarian stimulation, and polycystic ovaries. Increased production in obesity may cause menstrual irregularities and, in men, such signs of feminization as gynecomastia. Owing to the results seen with androstenedione overproduction, this test is useful in the diagnosis of menstrual irregularities, premature sexual development, and postmenopausal irregularities.",
