    "category": "Hematology & Coagulation",
    "description": "During hemostasis, a substance called thrombin stimulates the formation of fibrin from fibrinogen. This fibrin then forms a stable clot at the site of injury. Any excess amounts of clotting factors, which remain following hemostasis are inactivated by fibrin inhibitors which prevent clotting from occurring when it is not needed. One such substance is antithrombin III (AT-III).\n\nAT-III is a naturally occurring protein immunoglobulin that is synthesized by the liver. The action of AT-III is catalyzed by heparin. Its role is to inactivate thrombin and other coagulation factors, thus inhibiting the coagulation process. The proper balance between thrombin and AT-III allows for appropriate hemostasis to occur. If, however, this balance is disrupted, problems can arise. For example, if there is a congenital deficiency of AT-III, coagulation will not be inhibited at an adequate level, resulting in a hypercoagulability state with a high risk of thrombosis.",
