    "contributingFactors": "• Hemolysis of the blood sample will alter test results.\n• Exposure of the blood sample to sunlight or artificial light for 1 hour or more will decrease the bilirubin content of the sample.\n• Testing with contrast media within 24 hours will alter test results.\n• Drugs that may increase total bilirubin: allopurinal, anabolic steroids, antimalarials, ascorbic acid, azathioprine, chlorpropamide, cholinergics, codeine, dextran, diuretics, epinephrine, isoproterenol, levodopa, MAO inhibitors, meperidine, methyldopa, methotrexate, morphine, oral contraceptives, phenazopyridine, phenothiazines, quinidine, rifampin, streptomycin, theophylline, tyrosine, vitamin A.\n• Drugs that may decrease total bilirubin: barbiturates, caffeine, chlorine, citrate, corticosteroids, ethanol, penicillin, protein, salicylates, sulfonamides, urea.",
