    "description": "Chloride (Cl−) is the major anion of the extracellular fluid. Chloride levels have an inverse relationship with those of bicarbonate; thus, they reflect acid-base status. In individuals with too much base, urine chloride measurements help differentiate the cause of the problem: loss of salt through dehydration, vomiting, or use of diuretics (urinary chloride would be very low) or excess hormones such as cortisol or aldosterone (urinary chloride would be very high).\n\nChloride has several functions, including maintaining electrical neutrality by counterbalancing cations such as sodium (NaCl, HCl), acting as one component of the buffering system, aiding in digestion, and helping to maintain osmotic pressure and water balance. The amount of chloride excreted by the kidneys in a 24-hour period is an indication of the patient’s electrolyte balance and mirror the dietary intake of chloride and of sodium.",
