    "title": "Cholinesterase (Acetylcholinesterase, Cholinesterase  RBC, Pseudocholinesterase)",
    "aliases": "Acetylcholinesterase, Cholinesterase  RBC, Pseudocholinesterase",
    "pages": "P.174 – P.176",
    "category": "Hematology & Coagulation",
    "description": "There are two enzymes which hydrolyze acetylcholine (ACh): acetylcholinesterase, or true cholinesterase, and pseudocholinesterase, or serum cholinesterase. Acetylcholinesterase, which is present in nerve tissue, the spleen, and the gray matter of the brain, helps with the transmission of impulses across nerve endings to muscle fibers. Pseudocholinesterase, produced mainly in the liver, appears in small amounts in the pancreas, intestine, heart, and white matter of the brain.\n\nTwo groups of anticholinesterase chemicals, organophosphates and muscle relaxants, either affect or are affected by these enzymes. Organophosphates, which inactivate acetylcholinesterase, are found in many insecticides and nerve gas. Muscle relaxants, such as succinylcholine, are normally destroyed by pseudocholinesterase. If, however, there is a lack of pseudocholinesterase, the patient may experience a prolonged period of apnea if given muscle relaxants during surgery. Thus, patients who are to receive such drugs during surgery should be pretested for cholinesterase.",
