    "title": "Fetoscopy",
    "aliases": "",
    "pages": "P.274 – P.275",
    "category": "Imaging & Procedures",
    "description": "Fetoscopy is an endoscopic procedure which allows direct visualization of the fetus. The procedure can be done for diagnostic and/or therapeutic reasons. Fetoscopy is done to evaluate the fetus for birth defects and to collect fetal blood and tissue (skin) samples. Fetoscopy can also be done for therapeutic purposes such as laser occlusion of abnormal vessels. Because the procedure involves inserting the fetoscope through the abdominal wall into the amniotic cavity, there are serious risks, including miscarriage, bleeding, and preterm labor. Because of these risks, fetoscopy is done only if there is a high probability that the fetus is not normal or there is a strong family history of birth defects. Typically other testing such as fetal ultrasound, amniocentesis, or chorionic villus sampling are the preferred testing methods whenever possible.",
