    "aliases": "FSH",
    "pages": "P.283 – P.284",
    "category": "Endocrine & Hormones",
    "description": "This test is used in the diagnosis of hypogonadism, infertility, menstrual disorders, precocious puberty, and menopause. Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) is secreted by the anterior pituitary gland. During the follicular phase of the menstrual cycle, FSH initiates the production of estradiol by the follicle, with the two hormones working together to further develop the ovarian (egg) follicle. A midcycle surge of FSH and luteinizing hormone (LH) is followed by ovulation. During the luteal phase, FSH stimulates the production of progesterone which, along with estradiol, facilitates ovarian response to LH. When menopause occurs, the ovaries stop functioning, resulting in increased FSH levels. In men, FSH stimulates the testes to produce mature sperm and also promotes the production of androgen binding proteins.",
    "evidence": "In terms of infertility testing and management, elevated FSH levels on day 3 of the menstrual cycle have been correlated to poor performance with assisted reproductive technology.\n• Day 3 FSH values >15 µIU/L probably reflect poor future reproductive potential.\n• Day 3 FSH values <10 µIU/L represent normal follicular potential\n• Values of 10–15 µIU/L probably represent an effect of aging on fertility.",
