    "id": 160,
    "title": "Gallbladder Scan (Hepatobiliary Imaging, Hepatobiliary Scan with Cholecystokinin [CCK], HIDA Scan)",
    "aliases": "Hepatobiliary Imaging, Hepatobiliary Scan with Cholecystokinin [CCK], HIDA Scan",
    "pages": "P.288 – P.290",
    "category": "Imaging & Procedures",
    "description": "For a gallbladder scan, the patient is given a radionuclide compound, hydroxyl iminodiacetic acid (HIDA) labeled with technetium-99m via an IV injection. A scintillation camera is used to take a radioactivity reading from the body. These readings are fed into a computer, which translates these readings into a two-dimensional gray scale picture. These pictures are obtained at 15 to 30 minute intervals. If the biliary\n\nsystem has not visualized within 2 hours, the scan is repeated 2 to 4 hours later. This test is used in the diagnosis of cholecystitis. Delayed filling of the gallbladder is indicative of chronic or acalculous cholecystitis, whereas failure to visualize the gallbladder is diagnostic of an obstruction of the cystic duct, as in acute or calculus cholecystitis. If the HIDA enters the bile ducts but does not enter the small intestine, then an obstruction of the bile duct from stones or cancer is suspected.\n\nThe scan can also be done with an additional component. This involves administration of cholecystokinin (CCK). This substance causes the gallbladder to contract. This allows for estimation of the ejection fraction of the gallbladder, which is a measure of how much bile leaves the gallbladder during contraction.",
