    "description": "The nonstress test (NST) is a noninvasive technique used to evaluate the status of the fetus. Common reasons for an NST include: the patient having diabetes or hypertension, the fetus being small or not growing properly, and pregnancies extending past the due date.\n\nUnlike the contraction stress test, the NST does not include stimulation with oxytocin. The fetal activity monitored in this test may be spontaneous or induced by uterine contraction or external manipulation. Normally, the fetal heart rate (FHR) should accelerate in response to fetal movement. The fetus is reported as being “reactive” when two or more FHR accelerations are detected within a 20-minute period. Each of the accelerations must be at least 15 beats per minute and last for at least 15 seconds. The NST is highly reliable for determining fetal viability. Only with a “nonreactive” result, is a contraction stress test (CST) indicated.",
