    "id": 279,
    "title": "Prothrombin Fragment 1 +",
    "aliases": "F1 + 2",
    "pages": "P.490 – P.491",
    "category": "Hematology & Coagulation",
    "description": "During the process of hemostasis, intrinsic and extrinsic pathways lead to the activation of coagulation factor X. This leads to the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin. Thrombin then stimulates the formation of fibrin from fibrinogen. This fibrin, with the addition of fibrin stabilizing factor, forms a stable fibrin clot at the site of injury.\n\nDuring the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin, prothrombin fragment 1 + 2 (F 1 + 2) is produced. Thus, F 1 + 2 is considered an indicator of prethrombosis or a hypercoagulable state. It is during this prethrombotic time that intervention with anticoagulants can best prevent thrombosis from occurring. Since thrombin itself is not measurable, F 1+ 2 provides a marker of thrombin generation. F 1+ 2 can also be used to monitor the effectiveness of anticoagulants.",
