    "category": "Imaging & Procedures",
    "description": "The radioactive iodine uptake (RAIU) test evaluates thyroid function by measuring the amount of radioactive iodine (123I or 131I) that accumulates in the thyroid gland 6 and 24 hours after ingestion of the substance. A scanner measures the amount of radioactivity in the thyroid gland and, when compared with the original dose of radioactivity, provides a percentage of uptake. The RAIU is used in the diagnosis of hyperthyroidism and at times, hypothyroidism. It can also be used for evaluation of response to treatment/surgery for the conditions. The scan may also be useful in assessing the functional status of any palpable thyroid irregularities or nodules associated with a toxic goiter. Results of the RAIU are reviewed in conjunction with results of blood tests such as T3, T4, and TSH.",
