    "pages": "P.521 – P.522",
    "category": "Immunology & Serology",
    "description": "Rubella, also known as the German measles or 3-day measles, is a virus that is not usually considered a serious condition. It usually causes fever and transient rash in children and adults who contract it. Rubella is serious, however, if a woman contracts the disease during the first trimester of pregnancy. It can cause miscarriage, stillbirth, and congenital defects such as deafness, microcephaly, and heart defects. Thus, it is extremely important for women considering pregnancy to be testing for susceptibility or immunity to rubella.\n\nThe rubella antibody test measures IgG and IgM antibody formation against the rubella virus. It can be used to diagnose rubella and to determine whether the person is immune to the virus. A diagnosis of rubella is made when a fourfold increase between the acute titer and the convalescent titer occurs. Demonstration of specific IgG on a serum sample is evidence of immunity to rubella.",
