    "id": 310,
    "title": "Stool Culture/Stool for Ova & Parasites",
    "aliases": "",
    "pages": "P.542 – P.543",
    "category": "Immunology & Serology",
    "description": "The gastrointestinal (GI) tract contains many bacteria and fungi as its normal flora. When some of this normal flora is suppressed in some way, as with antibiotic use or immunosuppression, the remaining bacteria can become pathogenic. In addition, pathogens may enter the GI tract, causing symptoms such as persistent or bloody diarrhea, abdominal pain, and fever. Some pathogens cause symptoms directly and others, such as Clostridium difficile, cause symptoms through the toxins they produce.\n\nPeople may be exposed to enteric pathogens in a variety of ways. For example, exposure may occur when traveling outside of the United States or through dietary intake of undercooked food or contaminated food and water. It is important to identify the pathogens to assist with treatment planning and prevention of complications.\n\nstool culture is done to isolate and identify organisms which might be causing GI symptoms. The culture is also warranted if there are leukocytes (WBCs) in the stool. In most laboratories, routine stool cultures are limited to screening for Salmonella and Shigella species, and Campylobacter jejuni. Testing for E. coli may require a special order.\n\nStool for ova and parasites tests for the presence of a parasite or worm-like infection of the intestine, or ova, the egg stage of a parasite’s development. This test is indicated for patients who have recently traveled, those with persistent diarrhea, or when the diarrhea has been unresponsive to antimicrobial therapy. Testing typically enables identification of Giardia lamblia and Entamoeba histolytica.",
    "evidence": "Any illness involving diarrhea that persists for greater than 7 days, especially in an immunocompromised patient, should prompt further testing of stool specimens.",
    "normalValues": "- Normal intestinal flora\n- Negative for pathogens",
    "increased": [],
    "decreased": [],
    "abnormalFindings": [
      "Positive",
      "Bacterial enterocolitis",
      "Parasitic enterocolitis",
      "Protozoal enterocolitis"
    ],
    "contributingFactors": "Drugs that may cause false-negative results: antibiotics, barium, bismuth, mineral oil.",
    "clinicalAlerts": "• Patients complaining of diarrhea for at least 2 weeks usually have stool analysis performed, often including stool for WBCs, stool culture, stool for ova and parasites, Clostridium difficile, and possibly Giardia.\n• Follow-up stool analysis may be done if symptoms persist despite initial stool tests having negative results, or to demonstrate the pathogen is no longer present in the stool.",
