    "id": 331,
    "title": "Total Iron-Binding Capacity",
    "aliases": "TIBC",
    "pages": "P.573 – P.575",
    "category": "Hematology & Coagulation",
    "description": "Iron in the body is found in several locations. A large portion (approximately 65%) is carried in the red blood cells by hemoglobin. Another 4% is found in myoglobin in the skeletal muscle. Ferritin stores in the liver, bone marrow, and spleen account for almost 30% of the total iron. The remaining iron is in transit throughout the body, bound to transferrin and other iron binding serum proteins.\n\nTotal iron-binding capacity (TIBC) measures the total amount of iron that transferrin can bind. It is usually measured along with serum iron when investigating a possible iron deficiency anemia. When iron stores are low, the TIBC is usually higher than normal. The test can also be performed when iron overload is suspected. A calculation of serum iron divided by TIBC provides a percentage known as transferrin saturation, a useful indicator of iron status.",
