    "description": "Bilirubin, which is one of the components of bile, is formed in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. It is also formed as a result of hemoglobin breakdown. There are three types of bilirubin: total, direct (conjugated), and indirect (unconjugated).\n\nDirect, or conjugated, bilirubin is converted to urobilinogen by intestinal bacteria in the duodenum. The majority of urobilinogen is excreted in the stool. The liver reprocesses the remaining urobilinogen into bile. A very small amount is excreted in the urine.\n\nThe fecal urobilinogen level is dependent upon the amount of conjugated bilirubin excreted in the bile salts into the intestine. Fecal urobilinogen levels decrease when obstructive jaundice (as from gallstones) or hepatic jaundice occurs, since the bilirubin is unable to reach the intestines for excretion and instead enters the bloodstream for excretion by the kidneys.\n\nThere must also be adequate numbers of bacteria present in the intestine to breakdown the bilirubin into urobilinogen. With inadequate bacterial presence, as\n\noccurs following oral antibiotic therapy, the decreased urobilinogen in the feces results in a light-colored stool.",
