    "description": "A viral culture involves collecting a small sample of body tissue or fluid and placing it in a cell culture. The virus is allowed to replicate to help with identification of the specific virus. It is best for samples to be collected during the acute phase of a condition.\n\nSources of specimens for culturing include blood, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), respiratory secretions, stool, tissue, urine, eyes, and lesions of the oral mucosa, rectal mucosa, skin, and genitals. The types of viruses able to be detected include adenovirus, cytomegalovirus, herpes simplex virus, influenza, mumps, respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), varicella-zoster virus, and enteroviruses such as coxsackie virus and poliovirus. The length of time from specimen collection until diagnosis varies from hours to days, depending on the virus and the culture method used.",
